1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
The DuckDB, an open source fast and vetable SQL-O-Lap database that simplifies the way developers

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:11,279
implement analytics. It was developed in the Netherlands, written in C++, and first released in

3
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:17,280
2019. And the TLDR is that it's like SQL Lite, but for columnar data. Everybody knows that SQL

4
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:21,760
Lite is the most deployed database in the world, and that's because it runs as a single binary with

5
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:26,800
no dedicated database management server process necessary, allowing it to be embedded almost anywhere,

6
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:31,200
like the billions of smartphones around the world. DuckDB is built on the same philosophy,

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,679
but instead of storing data row-wise, it stores it column-wise. But why does that matter?

8
00:00:35,679 --> 00:00:40,640
Well, you see, storing rows together is great for transactional workloads like most apps out there,

9
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:45,040
like an e-commerce website where you need to read and write entire records at the same time.

10
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,760
But it's not so great for high volume time series data commonly found in analytics and IOT,

11
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:54,560
where you need to read and analyze all the data in one column across many records. When storage is

12
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:59,600
optimized for columns, it opens the door to much faster data aggregation, like calculating an

13
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:04,640
average, in addition to much faster filters and joins. And DuckDB achieves excellent performance

14
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:09,600
with massive data sets, thanks to its columnar vectorized query execution engine, where large

15
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:14,560
batches of values are processed as vectors in one operation in parallel. It's already in use at

16
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:19,280
big companies like Meta, Google, and Airbnb, and you can get started by installing it, then run

17
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,840
the DuckDB command from the terminal. And now we can start inserting data just like any other

18
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:29,040
SQL database. Then, if we want to read an individual column, we can do so with a select query.

19
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:33,680
And what's especially awesome is that if we have some existing data in a CSV or parquet file,

20
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:37,840
we can access it directly in a SQL statement. In addition, we could take that data,

21
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:43,200
then output it to a different format, like JSON or even an HTML table. That's cool and all,

22
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,439
but the real power of this database is its ability to perform aggregations when working with

23
00:01:47,439 --> 00:01:52,240
time series data. Like imagine we have a massive data set of stock prices, and need to calculate

24
00:01:52,239 --> 00:01:57,119
things like the average max and min values for the last day. We can easily achieve that in a

25
00:01:57,119 --> 00:02:02,239
select statement thanks to built-in aggregate functions. Then use Group-by to bucket the data into

26
00:02:02,239 --> 00:02:06,959
a specific time range, but under the hood we get excellent performance because unlike SQL

27
00:02:06,959 --> 00:02:12,479
Light, which processes data row by row, DuckDB processes data in vectorized batches of rows,

28
00:02:12,479 --> 00:02:16,959
and is multi-threaded by default, making it an excellent choice for analytical workloads.

29
00:02:16,959 --> 00:02:21,519
This has been DuckDB in 100 seconds. Thanks for watching and I will see you in the next one.

